> # llama.cpp fork: 1.7x to 2.4x faster decode on MoE models bigger than your VRAM

The newest mixture-of-experts models (GLM-5.3-Flash, MiMo-V2.6-Flash, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next, Qwen3.6) are far bigger than a gaming GPU.
This fork keeps their experts in RAM and turns the free VRAM into a live cache of the experts the model is using; the GPUs compute
the cached ones and the CPU the rest, at the same time. No special switches needed.

```sh
llama-server -m GLM-5.3-Flash-GSQ-RCO-3.0bit-q4kattn.gguf
llama ... (truncated)
Thank you for sharing the LLAMA fork's performance comparison with the stock llama.cpp on different machines. Here's a summary of the key findings:

1. The fork shows significant performance improvements when a MoE model's size is within the VRAM capacity. The decode speedup is largest when a third to a half of the model fits in VRAM (1.9x to 2.4x).
2. The gain over stock llama.cpp is the share of the model that fits in VRAM (decode).
3. The performance of the fork is comparable to stock llama.cpp on machines with sufficient VRAM, such as Machine A with 2x RTX 3090 and Machine D with 4x RTX 3090.
4. The performance on Machine B, a laptop with an RTX 4060 and Ryzen 9 8945HS, is lower than the other machines, but the decode speedup is still significant (1.7x

